### Reject icon indices that overflow the native signed range

**Severity:** `high`

The managed parser currently accepts values above `INT_MAX` because it only rejects the unsigned sentinel. Inputs such as `2147483648` wrap to `int.MinValue` at the cast and no longer match the native converter's behavior. Reject values above `int.MaxValue` before the cast, and please add the matching accepted/rejected boundary rows in `IconPathParserTests`.

```suggestion
        if (result == UnsignedConversionError || result > int.MaxValue)
        {
            return SignedConversionError;
        }
```

### Verify that extracted icons stay visible as well as transparent

**Severity:** `medium`

This test currently passes even if conversion returns a fully transparent bitmap, so it does not prove that the new extraction path preserved visible icon content after rebuilding alpha. Copy the pixels and assert that the result contains both transparent and nontransparent pixels.

```suggestion
        Assert.AreEqual(BitmapPixelFormat.Bgra8, bitmap.BitmapPixelFormat);
        Assert.AreEqual(BitmapAlphaMode.Premultiplied, bitmap.BitmapAlphaMode);

        var pixels = new byte[bitmap.PixelWidth * bitmap.PixelHeight * 4];
        bitmap.CopyToBuffer(System.Runtime.InteropServices.WindowsRuntime.WindowsRuntimeBufferExtensions.AsBuffer(pixels));
        Assert.IsTrue(
            Enumerable.Range(0, pixels.Length / 4).Any(pixel => pixels[(pixel * 4) + 3] == 0),
            "Extracted icons should preserve transparent pixels.");
        Assert.IsTrue(
            Enumerable.Range(0, pixels.Length / 4).Any(pixel => pixels[(pixel * 4) + 3] != 0),
            "Extracted icons should preserve visible pixels.");
```
